🐛 Describe the bug
In c10::TensorImpl::raw_mutable_data ( https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/v1.12.1/c10/core/TensorImpl.h#L1805 ) memory with non-primitive dtype gets initialized via:
data_type_.placementNew()(storage_.data(), numel_);
However, when the storage is located on a different device (i.e. GPU), then it's not possible to run the host's placementNew() method on that data. We ran into this issue when allocating data for c10::complex<float> and c10::complex<double> dtypes.
The placementNew runs the following code on the host, but using a device ptr, which causes a segfault.
template <typename T>
inline void _PlacementNew(void* ptr, size_t n) {
  T* typed_ptr = static_cast<T*>(ptr);
  for (const auto i : c10::irange(n)) {
    new (typed_ptr + i) T;
  }
}
A reproducing code fragment (you can use any non-CPU device type)
auto meta	= caffe2::TypeMeta::Make<c10::complex<float>>();
auto K		= c10::DispatchKey::VE;
auto D		= c10::DeviceType::VE;
auto allocator	= c10::GetAllocator(D);

// create empty storage, so the Tensor is forced to allocate new memory when resizing
auto storage = c10::make_intrusive<c10::StorageImpl>(
	c10::StorageImpl::use_byte_size_t(), 0, allocator->allocate(0), allocator, true);

// create tensor
auto th = new c10::TensorImpl(storage, c10::DispatchKeySet(K), meta);

// resize
size_t shape[] = {1, 2, 100};
th->Resize(c10::IntArrayRef(shape, 3));

// trigger segfault
th->raw_mutable_data(meta);
Relevant stack trace:
#0  0x00007fffe7410815 in void caffe2::detail::_PlacementNew<c10::complex<float> >(void*, unsigned long) ()
   from /home/xxx/.local/lib/python3.7/site-packages/torch/lib/libc10.so
#1  0x00007fffcc15a354 in c10::TensorImpl::raw_mutable_data(caffe2::TypeMeta) ()

